WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:04.550
Ashton Kutcher first became a household name
thanks to his breakthrough role as the lovable

00:00:04.550 --> 00:00:07.910
doofus Michael Kelso on the hit sitcom, That
'70s Show.

00:00:07.910 --> 00:00:12.389
But he's also attempted to gain some traction
on the big screen — with mixed results.

00:00:12.389 --> 00:00:16.190
Lately, Kutcher's movie roles have seemed
fewer and farther in between.

00:00:16.190 --> 00:00:19.920
With that in mind, here are some theories
as to why Hollywood doesn't have much room

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:21.859
for him at the box office anymore...

00:00:21.859 --> 00:00:23.759
Small screen start

00:00:23.759 --> 00:00:28.019
Viewers might have been split between Team
Kelso and Team Hyde when it came to Jackie's

00:00:28.019 --> 00:00:33.010
love life throughout That '70s Show, but Kutcher's
on-screen presence as the jovial bonehead

00:00:33.010 --> 00:00:34.690
simply could not be denied.

00:00:34.690 --> 00:00:36.030
(audience laughs)

00:00:36.030 --> 00:00:37.360
"Woahoho!"

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:39.520
Ya ah hehe"

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:40.020
(audience laughs)

00:00:40.030 --> 00:00:44.580
The show catapulted the then-21-year-old into
instant fame, and he went on to solidify his

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:49.540
status as a small-screen A-lister by co-creating
MTV's prank show Punk'd, where his rolodex

00:00:49.540 --> 00:00:53.380
full of famous people came in handy as he
pulled practical jokes on all his new Hollywood

00:00:53.380 --> 00:00:54.380
pals.

00:00:54.380 --> 00:00:59.760
He even single-handedly brought mesh trucker
hats back into style, which is no easy feat.

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:02.510
Dude, where's my blockbuster?

00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:07.070
Kutcher did have some initial success translating
his slapstick humor to the big screen in 2000's

00:01:07.070 --> 00:01:12.210
Dude, Where's My Car?, and he charmed alongside
Brittany Murphy in 2003's Just Married.

00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:17.600
But both films still earned scathing reviews
that didn't exactly bolster his rep as a serious

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:18.100
actor.

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:18.800
"And thennnn?"

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:19.300
"No!

00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:20.780
No and then!"

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:21.520
"And then?"

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:22.080
"No and then!"

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:22.580
"And then!"

00:01:22.580 --> 00:01:23.080
"No and then!"

00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:23.580
"And then!"

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:24.080
"No and then!"

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:29.560
Kutcher tried to turn things around with a
starring role in the 2004 time-travel thriller

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:30.560
The Butterfly Effect.

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:34.289
But while the movie made a nice chunk of change,
his Rotten Tomatoes average wasn't exactly

00:01:34.289 --> 00:01:37.799
improved as a result of the arguably underrated
flick.

00:01:37.799 --> 00:01:43.479
Collectively, Kutcher's filmography has been
marred with one pan after another from reviewers,

00:01:43.479 --> 00:01:47.319
and none of the movies he's acted in have
ever even broken the 50 percent threshold

00:01:47.319 --> 00:01:48.839
in their Rotten Tomatoes scores.

00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:51.720
"I gotta hand it to him he got me there hehe.

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:53.280
That's a major burn!

00:01:53.289 --> 00:01:54.689
Hehe."

00:01:54.689 --> 00:01:57.079
Total typecast

00:01:57.079 --> 00:02:00.529
Some of Kutcher's biggest commercial successes
on the silver screen have remained in the

00:02:00.529 --> 00:02:05.719
comedic realm, including his 2008 rom-com
with Cameron Diaz, What Happens in Vegas,

00:02:05.719 --> 00:02:10.350
his part in Garry Marshall's 2010 ensemble
dramedy Valentine's Day, and his "friends

00:02:10.350 --> 00:02:14.810
with benefits" film with Natalie Portman,
2011's No Strings Attached.

00:02:14.810 --> 00:02:19.001
In each of those movies, Kutcher's quirky
demeanor helped his characters stick, and

00:02:19.001 --> 00:02:23.610
although none of them were quite as dimwitted
as Michael Kelso or his Dude persona Jesse

00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:27.330
Montgomery III, they also weren't exactly
Mensa material.

00:02:27.330 --> 00:02:32.890
Put simply: his fans seem to prefer when Kutcher
sticks to the most basic levels of intelligence

00:02:32.890 --> 00:02:33.890
on-screen.

00:02:33.890 --> 00:02:39.340
His lead role in 2013's Jobs, a much-hyped
Steve Jobs biopic, failed to resonate with

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:44.410
audiences or critics as well, proving that
without the laughs, no one's really interested.

00:02:44.410 --> 00:02:46.130
Costly comeback

00:02:46.130 --> 00:02:50.280
As his film momentum slowed, Kutcher made
what was poised to be a "winning" return to

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:54.500
television in 2011, replacing Charlie Sheen
on Two and a Half Men.

00:02:54.500 --> 00:02:58.540
Despite the fact that Kutcher seemed like
an ideal addition to the show, which boasted

00:02:58.540 --> 00:03:03.220
sky-high viewership during Sheen's tenure,
ratings rapidly declined.

00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:08.180
After that, he again returned to TV with the
Netflix sitcom The Ranch in 2016.

00:03:08.180 --> 00:03:13.500
On paper, The Ranch had all the right ingredients
for small screen success — up to and including

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:18.680
his old screen partner from That '70s Show,
Danny Masterson, and a round table cameo from

00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:21.650
fellow former castmate Wilmer Valderrama.

00:03:21.650 --> 00:03:25.770
But unlike a lot of Netflix productions, it's
been met with little critical acclaim and

00:03:25.770 --> 00:03:29.460
hasn't attracted the kind of cult audience
enjoyed by many of the streaming service's

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:31.730
other original series.

00:03:31.730 --> 00:03:33.230
FFBF

00:03:33.230 --> 00:03:37.710
Another hindrance to Kutcher's film career
is, ironically, his offscreen success.

00:03:37.710 --> 00:03:41.900
He moonlights as a tech developer and investor,
which has become a serious second work life

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:42.900
for him.

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:46.980
And he basically helped put Twitter on the
map by engaging in a popularity contest on

00:03:46.980 --> 00:03:50.670
the platform with national news station CNN
in 2009.

00:03:50.670 --> 00:03:52.110
Plus, let's face it.

00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:56.569
He tends to make more headlines for his personal
life than his onscreen accomplishments.

00:03:56.569 --> 00:04:00.560
Kutcher and his first wife, Demi Moore, were
regular characters on the red carpet and used

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:04.410
their social network platforms to document
virtually every detail of their marriage,

00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:08.880
and he and his second wife, Mila Kunis, continue
to attract off-camera attention.

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:10.040
"We're making people.

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:11.590
We're just making people."

00:04:11.590 --> 00:04:12.090
"Ya."

00:04:12.090 --> 00:04:16.660
Even as audiences avoid Kutcher's films, a
lot of people still take the time to speculate

00:04:16.660 --> 00:04:19.030
on what the couple will name their children.

00:04:19.030 --> 00:04:21.930
Not that he doesn't give them plenty of reasons
to play along...

00:04:21.930 --> 00:04:27.760
"I, there was a rallying cry from a collective
that believed in the name Hawkeye."

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:29.460
"Hawkeye!"

00:04:29.660 --> 00:04:32.460
"I'm an Iowan, right, and a kid named Hawkeye."

00:04:32.470 --> 00:04:33.470
"Hawkeye!"

00:04:33.470 --> 00:04:35.790
"And it's like M*A*S*H, like he could be a
doctor!"

00:04:35.790 --> 00:04:39.740
Maybe the two should go ahead and just make
that fake MoonQuake Lake movie from Annie

00:04:39.740 --> 00:04:42.550
with Rihanna after all to turn things around
for him …

00:04:43.580 --> 00:04:46.320
"What if when you go to the moon there's an
astroid or something?"

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:49.320
"Then I would die."

00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:51.440
"But I will catch you."

00:04:51.450 --> 00:04:52.900
"I know you will."

00:04:52.900 --> 00:04:53.900
"It's time."

00:04:53.900 --> 00:04:54.900
Thanks for watching!

00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:57.190
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:04:57.190 --> 00:04:59.240
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

